WEBVTT

NOTE Part 3 journey. Subtitle times are estimates. cue-008 spans the code and memory beats on purpose.

cue-001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
A variable is a named place that holds a value while a program runs.

cue-002
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.760
We start with a simple picture: a box labeled score that currently holds ten.

cue-003
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.040
That picture is useful, but it is not how the computer actually sees the assignment.

cue-004
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.120
In source code, the same idea is written as score equals ten.

cue-005
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.620
The identifier on the left is the name we chose; the number on the right is the
value we stored.

cue-006
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:35.100
When this line runs, the language runtime looks up that name in memory.

cue-007
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.600
Memory is just a table of slots: an address, a name, and the value that lives there.

cue-008
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.100
The name score is the same in the code and in the table; only the representation
changes.

cue-009
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.380
Zooming in, the value cell is where ten actually sits.

cue-010
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:58.880
Zooming back out, the rest of the diagram is still there, and the clock never reset.

cue-011
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.380
So a variable is not the number itself. It is a stable name for a replaceable slot
in memory.
